---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import CopyCode from "../../components/CopyCode.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { id: "two-processes", label: "Two processes, one build" },
  { id: "who-does-what", label: "Who does what" },
  { id: "a-run", label: "A run, step by step" },
  { id: "commands", label: "Commands & tool wrappers" },
  { id: "typescript", label: "TypeScript builds" },
  { id: "python", label: "A Python build" },
  { id: "zuke-json", label: "zuke.json & the launcher" },
  { id: "own-sdk", label: "Writing an SDK" },
  { id: "under-the-hood", label: "Under the hood" },
];

const pythonSnippet = `from zuke import Build, parameter, run, target
from zuke.tools.docker import DockerTasks


class CI(Build):
    tag = parameter("image tag").default("dev")

    lint = target().description("ruff").executes(lambda ctx: ctx.sh("ruff", "check", "."))
    test = target().depends_on(lint).executes(lambda ctx: ctx.sh("pytest", "-q"))
    image = (
        target()
        .depends_on(test)
        .executes(
            lambda ctx: DockerTasks.build(
                ctx, lambda s: s.tag(f"app:{tag.value}").file("Dockerfile")
            )
        )
    )


run(CI)`;

const zukeJsonSnippet = `{
  "name": "App",
  "sdk": { "command": ["uv", "run", "build.py"] },
  "engine": "1.4.0"
}`;

const runSnippet = `./zuke --list        # the targets, as build.py describes them
./zuke test          # lint, then test — every command spawned by the engine
./zuke generate-ci   # the CI files the build declares
./zuke mcp           # the build as typed tools for an agent`;

const helloSnippet = `→ {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"hello","params":{"sdk":"zuke-python","protocol":1,"env":{…},"cwd":"/app"}}
← {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"build.describe"}
→ {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"name":"CI","targets":[…],"parameters":[…],…}}
← {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"build.configure","params":{"parameters":{"tag":"dev"}}}
← {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"target.run","params":{"target":"lint","runId":"…","dryRun":false}}
→ {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":10,"method":"exec","params":{"target":"lint","argv":["ruff","check","."]}}
← {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":10,"result":{"code":0,"stdout":"","stderr":""}}
→ {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"result":{}}
…
← {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"run.exit","params":{"code":0}}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Engine & workers"
  description="How a Zuke build runs: one engine does the work, and a thin worker — your build file, in TypeScript or Python — describes the build and runs the target bodies when asked."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead">
    A Zuke build is typed code in the language of your project. What runs it is
    always the same thing: the Zuke <strong>engine</strong>. Your build file is a
    <strong>worker</strong> — it tells the engine what the targets are and runs a
    target's body when the engine asks. Everything else happens in the engine,
    once, for every language. TypeScript builds run on it, Python is the
    first language SDK, and more language SDKs are coming on the same contract.
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-processes">Two processes, one build</h2>
  <p>
    When you run <code>./zuke test</code> in a Python project, two processes
    start. The launcher starts the engine. The engine starts your
    <code>build.py</code> and the two talk over a private connection on your
    machine for the rest of the run.
  </p>
  <div class="flow" aria-label="The launcher starts the engine, the engine starts the worker, and the two talk over a local socket">
    <div class="flow__box">
      <span class="flow__k">you</span>
      <code>./zuke test</code>
      <span class="flow__d">the launcher</span>
    </div>
    <span class="flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div class="flow__box flow__box--engine">
      <span class="flow__k">engine</span>
      <code>@zuke/engine</code>
      <span class="flow__d">plans the graph, runs every command, keeps the state</span>
    </div>
    <span class="flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">⇄</span>
    <div class="flow__box">
      <span class="flow__k">worker</span>
      <code>build.py</code>
      <span class="flow__d">describes the targets, runs their bodies</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    The split is deliberate. The hard parts of a build system — ordering,
    retries, timeouts, cancellation, caching, locks, resumable runs, service
    lifecycles, CI generation, the MCP server, the output — are written once, in
    the engine. A language SDK only has to do two small things: describe the
    build and run a body. That is what makes a new language cheap to add, and
    what guarantees a Python build and a TypeScript build behave identically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-does-what">Who does what</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>The engine</th><th>The worker (your build file)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Parses the command line, resolves <a href="/docs/parameters">parameters</a>, and plans the graph in topological order.</td>
        <td>Hands the engine a <em>manifest</em>: the targets, their dependencies, retries, timeouts, inputs and outputs, the parameters, the groups, the services, and the CI files it declares.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Runs every subprocess: argv only, no shell, output captured and <a href="/docs/secrets">redacted</a>, dry-run, timeout and cancel handled in one place.</td>
        <td>Runs a target's body when told to, and asks the engine to run each command on its behalf.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Owns the <a href="/docs/caching">cache</a>, the <a href="/docs/state">durable run records</a>, <a href="/docs/locks">locks</a>, resume, and <a href="/docs/services">service</a> start, readiness and stop.</td>
        <td>Answers the small questions only the build can: does this target's condition hold, what is its extra cache key, is this service ready.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Loads and runs every typed <a href="/docs/tools">tool wrapper</a> — Docker, npm, Deno, Git, the cloud CLIs — on the worker's behalf.</td>
        <td>Calls a wrapper through a generated, typed facade in its own language; the engine runs the real thing.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Renders the <a href="/docs/console">build log</a> and summary, generates CI, serves the <a href="/docs/mcp">MCP server</a>.</td>
        <td>Logs lines; even a stray <code>print</code> arrives in the log rather than on the wire.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="a-run">A run, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Start.</strong> <code>./zuke test</code> reads <code>zuke.json</code>,
      finds the engine version and the worker command, and starts the engine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hello.</strong> The engine opens a private connection on your
      machine and starts the worker with its address and a one-time token. The
      worker connects, says which SDK it is, and sends its environment and
      working directory — so a <code>uv run</code> virtualenv is what every
      later command sees.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Describe.</strong> The engine asks for the manifest. From here on
      the targets, the <code>--list</code> output, the <code>--help</code>, the
      graph and the generated CI are all the engine's, with the build's data in
      them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Configure.</strong> The engine resolves parameters from flags,
      environment and defaults and tells the worker the values, so
      <code>tag.value</code> is set before any body runs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run.</strong> For each target in order the engine checks the cache,
      takes the lock, asks the condition, and tells the worker to run the body.
      While the body runs, each <code>ctx.sh(…)</code> or tool call is a request
      back to the engine, which spawns it and answers with the result. Retries,
      timeouts and cancellation apply to the body exactly as to a TypeScript
      target.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Finish.</strong> The engine records the run, prints the summary,
      tells the worker the run is over, and waits for it to exit. If the engine
      itself dies, the worker's connection ends and it exits on its own; the
      engine never leaves a build file running.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="commands">Commands &amp; tool wrappers</h2>
  <p>
    A body never spawns a process itself. <code>ctx.sh("pytest", "-q")</code>
    sends the argv to the engine, which runs it the way the <code>$</code> shell
    does in TypeScript: as discrete arguments, never a shell string, so there is
    nothing to inject into; output is streamed to the log and captured; secrets
    are redacted; <code>--dry-run</code> prints instead of running; a timeout
    or a cancel kills the process. One implementation, every language.
  </p>
  <p>
    The typed tool wrappers work the same way. The engine publishes a
    machine-readable spec of every <code>@zuke/*</code> wrapper — its tasks,
    their settings, every setter — and an SDK generates its facades from it.
    <code>DockerTasks.build(ctx, lambda s: s.tag("app:dev"))</code> in Python
    sends the engine the wrapper's name and release, the task, and the setters
    the lambda called. The engine loads that exact wrapper release and runs it
    with the worker's environment and directory. So the whole catalogue of
    wrappers is available in every language from day one, at the same version
    pinned for everyone.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      A call the engine cannot satisfy is refused by name — the wrapper, the
      task, or the setter it could not find — rather than failing somewhere
      inside. A task that throws fails the call with its message, and a cancel
      cuts it short.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="typescript">TypeScript builds</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>zuke.ts</code> build is the same model with one process fewer.
    <code>@zuke/core</code> <em>is</em> the engine, as a library: a TypeScript
    build imports it, and <code>run(MyBuild)</code> hosts the build in-process
    with no socket in between. The <code>@zuke/engine</code> package is that
    same core with a worker host in front of it, for a build written in another
    language. Every feature on this site — caching, locks, resume, services,
    canary releases, the MCP server, AI review — is core, so it reaches both
    kinds of build the day it ships.
  </p>

  <h2 id="python">A Python build</h2>
  <p>
    Python is the first SDK. A build is a class whose fields are targets;
    dependencies are references the type checker sees; each body gets a
    <code>ctx</code> that runs commands through the engine; and every tool
    wrapper has a typed facade under <code>zuke.tools</code>.
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={pythonSnippet} lang="python" />
  <p>
    Beside it, <code>zuke.json</code> names the worker command and the exact
    engine release that runs it:
  </p>
  <CopyCode code={zukeJsonSnippet} lang="json" />
  <p>Then the familiar surface, served by the engine:</p>
  <CopyCode code={runSnippet} lang="bash" multi />
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Get started.</strong> The SDK is
      <a href="https://pypi.org/project/zuke/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>zuke</code> on PyPI</a>,
      built at <a href="https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke-python" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">zuke-build/zuke-python</a>,
      and its worker passes every conformance case of the engine it pins.
      <code>zuke setup --sdk python</code> scaffolds a project; the
      <a href="/docs/python">Python quickstart</a> walks through it, and the
      <a href="/api/python">Python API reference</a> documents every class and
      facade. Python is the first SDK, not the last: more language SDKs are
      coming, each one a thin worker over the same engine.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="zuke-json">zuke.json &amp; the launcher</h2>
  <p>
    A project with a <code>zuke.ts</code> runs it directly. A project with no
    <code>zuke.ts</code> holds a build another SDK hosts, and its
    <code>zuke.json</code> says how: <code>sdk.command</code> is the worker's
    argv, and <code>engine</code> is the exact <code>@zuke/engine</code> release
    to run it with — <code>"1.4.0"</code>, never a range, so the engine cannot
    float between two runs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each SDK runs on its own stack only: a Python build never needs Deno. It
    runs on the standalone <code>zuke</code> binary, which carries the engine
    and every tool wrapper. The <code>./zuke</code> and <code>zuke.ps1</code>
    launchers pin one <code>zuke</code> release, with the SHA-256 of each
    platform's archive. On first use they download it from the GitHub release,
    verify it and keep it under <code>~/.zuke</code>, so a CI runner needs only
    the SDK's own toolchain. The global <code>zuke</code> command runs the
    build the same way from anywhere inside the project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A pin the installed <code>zuke</code> cannot serve goes to the project's
    launcher, which runs the release it pins. With no launcher, it is refused
    with how to get a <code>zuke</code> that serves it; it never falls back to
    another stack. The launchers and the pin move together, with
    <code>zuke upgrade --engine [&lt;version&gt;]</code>, never by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="own-sdk">Writing an SDK</h2>
  <p>
    The engine is built to host a build in any language, and the contract an
    SDK has to meet is published as data, not prose:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Conformance cases.</strong> Under
      <a href="https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke/tree/master/packages/engine/conformance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>packages/engine/conformance</code></a>
      each case is one manifest, one command line, and the exact calls the
      engine makes in order — retries, conditions, cache keys, locks, services,
      hooks, suspend and resume, a worker that crashes mid-body, an engine that
      dies. The engine replays them against a scripted worker in its own test
      suite, and an SDK replays them against its real worker. Passing every case
      is what "speaks the protocol" means.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The tool spec.</strong>
      <a href="https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke/tree/master/packages/engine/spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>packages/engine/spec/tools.json</code></a>
      describes every wrapper's tasks and settings, so an SDK generates its typed
      facades instead of writing them by hand — and regenerates them when it
      vendors a newer spec.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Versioning.</strong> A protocol change lands in the engine first.
      An SDK pins the engine release it was tested against and moves forward
      deliberately.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="under-the-hood">Under the hood</h2>
  <p>
    For the curious, the whole transport fits in a paragraph. The engine listens
    on loopback TCP and accepts exactly one connection. It starts the worker with
    three environment variables — the address, a per-run token, and the protocol
    version — and the two exchange newline-delimited
    <a href="https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JSON-RPC 2.0</a>
    messages. The worker's own stdout and stderr are piped to the engine as log
    lines, so the socket is the only channel that carries protocol. The worker
    ignores <kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd>; the engine drives shutdown, so a cancelled run is
    one coordinated stop rather than two processes racing to die.
  </p>
  <p>A trimmed transcript of the Python build above:</p>
  <Code code={helloSnippet} lang="text" theme="night-owl" />
  <p>
    Engine-to-worker calls are <code>build.describe</code>,
    <code>build.configure</code>, the <code>build.on*</code> hooks the manifest
    flagged, <code>target.run</code>, <code>target.condition</code>,
    <code>target.cacheKey</code>, <code>target.settings</code>,
    <code>target.validate</code>, <code>service.start</code>,
    <code>service.ready</code>, <code>service.stop</code>, and finally
    <code>run.exit</code> (or <code>run.cancel</code>). Worker-to-engine calls
    are <code>exec</code>, <code>tool.call</code>, <code>log</code>,
    <code>state.get</code>/<code>set</code>/<code>trySet</code>,
    <code>secret.register</code> and <code>signals</code> — the
    <a href="/docs/run-context">run context</a> a body already has in
    TypeScript, served over the wire. The full shapes, with the guard that
    rejects each malformed field by name, are in the
    <a href="https://jsr.io/@zuke/engine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>@zuke/engine</code></a>
    API docs.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>

